{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall make atonement for her, and she shall be clean.</b> This atonement is for the earlier sin of Eve, which caused her the pain of childbirth, and out of her pain she perhaps flung words upward, saying: If so, why then am I pregnant with toil and suffering? And a proof from what is written: ״וְטָהֲרָה מִמְּקוֹר דָּמֶיהָ״ — from that source sin and menstruation were opened up, for it caused her all this, and this is the sin of Eve as stated. Also the expression ״דָּמֶיהָ״ includes the sin, from the expression ״דְּמֵיהֶם בָּם״ (Leviticus 20:27)." "And some say that this atonement is for the oath, that she swore not to attend to her husband from the hour she bends down to give birth (Niddah 31b); therefore the one who gives birth to a male regrets quickly out of abundant joy, therefore her atonement comes quickly, but the one who gives birth to a female suffers pain and does not regret quickly, therefore her atonement is delayed. And even though the oath does not take effect at all — for if not so, how is she permitted to her husband after seven [days] for a male and fourteen for a female without atonement, since the bringing of the atonement is after forty [days] for a male and eighty for a female? For in truth there is no oath here, for the matter is not in her hand, since she is subjugated to her husband, and it is like one who swears that he does not wish to pay his debt. Nevertheless she needs atonement because she brought forth from her mouth the oath and reflected in her heart to call quarrel against HaShem out of her pain. Therefore her offering is a sin-offering, to atone for the sin of the deed, for the pursing of her lips is counted as a deed, and a burnt-offering for the reflection of the heart. And there is a kind of hint to this from what is written: ״וְטָהֲרָה מִמְּקוֹר דָּמֶיהָ״ — for if she swore in her anger, then surely the seething of the blood was the cause of this, for it is the cause of all anger, and all anger has its source in the blood and its seething; therefore it says: ״וְטָהֲרָה מִמְּקוֹר דָּמֶיהָ״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse citation לויקרא כ:כז is normalized to Leviticus 20:27.", 1 "Resolved ״ה׳״ as HaShem per divine-name rules. The Talmudic citation נדה לא: is rendered as Niddah 31b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:02.253468028Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}